        ":question/problem": "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-3x?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x \\\\  & =-3(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x \\\\  & =-3(1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-3).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-3x.$ \n\n![](images/08/08e854b04d47ff670b197a2a0c7b51c13eb135626e0ed6cdfd24368d4032f259.png)"
